# Lore drafts (canon: soft): places in and beyond the academy.

@lore the_village
keys: village, barge, market square, market day, tailor, pawnbroker
canon: soft
text: The village lies across the lake, reached by barge. On its square stand the bakery's trestle, the tailor's rail of robes and the pawnbroker's creaking sign; on market days Mir keeps a stall there. Round the square are the Gilded Newt, Mabel's tea shop, Grimsby's Books and Pell & Daughters, and their shopkeepers have seen every kind of student.

@lore gilded_newt
keys: Gilded Newt, tavern, landlady
canon: soft
text: The village tavern, with low beams and a fire that never goes out. By day it's a few regulars and the landlady with a cloth over her shoulder; by night it's all elbows and cider. Students work shifts there and card games run late.

@lore mabels
keys: Mabel's, tea shop
canon: soft
text: Forty kinds of tea and three kinds of cake. Mabel reads your mood from the way you open the door.

@lore grimsbys
keys: Grimsby's Books, bookshop
canon: soft
text: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.

@lore pell_and_daughters
keys: Pell & Daughters, apothecary, Old Pell
canon: soft
text: The village apothecary, with a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters keep the counter now, and he counts aloud behind them. It buys what foragers bring out of the Whisperwood.

@lore bakery
keys: bakery, baker's daughter
canon: soft
text: The same family has baked in the village for two hundred years. A great-great-great-grandmother waited all her life for a letter from a student who died.

@lore whisperwood
keys: Whisperwood, forest, moonpetal, shadow stag, heartwood, waystone
canon: soft
text: An old forest west of the lake that talks in its sleep. The path through it is safe; off it, the wood goes down and down: the fringe, the thicket, the red-barked heartwood, and at the bottom the Hush. Foragers leave offerings at a worn waystone on the way in, and Pell buys what they bring out. A stag made of shadow walks the deep wood and steps aside only for the brave; Professor Brask bows to it in the bracken.

@lore secret_garden
keys: secret garden, silver bees, humming pear tree
canon: soft
text: Behind the potting shed, a door in the wall leads to Maud's secret garden: glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.

@lore greenhouses
keys: greenhouses, moonflower
canon: soft
text: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers there bloom once every eleven years and fill the greenhouse with silver light.

@lore observatory
keys: observatory, telescope, east tower
canon: soft
text: A brass dome atop the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes. On cold nights Kael sits on the roof outside it, and frost spreads around him.

@lore boathouse
keys: boathouse, jetty
canon: soft
text: An old boathouse with a jetty over the water, where people say the things they can't say indoors. The tunnel to the round door starts here.

@lore thornwick
keys: Thornwick Academy, rival school
canon: soft
text: The rival academy across the hills, famous for illusionists and tournament grudges. Exchange students come and go with suspicious politeness.

@lore the_north
keys: the North, Northern, ice palaces, singing glaciers, northern lights
canon: soft
text: The frozen North of ice palaces, singing glaciers and green-and-violet lights in the sky. Northerners touch foreheads in farewell and practise a frost-craft older than anything taught at Lumenhold.

@lore port_calder
keys: Port Calder, coast, street origin
canon: soft
text: A rough harbour town on the coast, where students of the street origin learned their trade and where trunks get stolen only once per visit.

@lore conclave
keys: Conclave, Conclave of the Seven Towers
canon: soft
text: The governing body of magic, in the capital. It asks professors for recommendations and runs laboratories where new spells are tested behind thick walls.

@lore capital_institutions
keys: Royal Library, Guild of Alchemists, Office of Unseen Affairs, Travelling Circus of Wonders
canon: soft
text: Beyond the academy lie the Royal Library's archives, the Guild of Alchemists' works, the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, which has no sign and no address, and the Travelling Circus of Wonders, which recruits graduates who want to wander.

# World expansion 1: the Whisperwood delve (content/events/whisperwood/)

@lore the_hush
keys: the Hush, Hush
canon: soft
text: The deepest part of the Whisperwood, past the heartwood, which wakes only at midnight. Its moonpetal blooms by the clearing-full, and its silence is a thing you can walk into.

@lore the_spinner
keys: the Spinner, Tuppence, Spinner, Spinner silk
canon: soft
text: A spider as big as a carriage, grey, with a voice like a cello, who rules the Hush and keeps worse things out of it. Old Maud found her as a hand-sized hatchling in the greenhouses in her first winter as groundskeeper, called her Tuppence, and let her go into the wood when she outgrew the potting shed. She has a blind left eye and receives guests on her left side, as is proper. Her oldest web holds sixty years of things foragers dropped, and her grey silk drinks the light.

@lore absalom_vrell
keys: Absalom Vrell, the Outcast, Vrell
canon: soft
text: A wizard expelled from Lumenhold for forbidden practice before the current students' parents were born; his name is still in the school ledger, crossed out. He waits in the Hush, by a lantern, with a flask of good tea, for the Spinner to fall. He taught the thornwitch and the masked dabblers in the wood. A grandmaster of every forbidden spell, courteous and bored; nobody has challenged him in forty years. He is not part of any plot but his own.

@lore thornwitch
keys: thornwitch, thorn wand
canon: soft
text: A hedge-practitioner in a cloak sewn with thorns who drinks the life of trees in the Whisperwood's thicket with Siphon, and offers apprentices a blackthorn wand.

@lore whisperwood_creatures
keys: bramble ferret, bristleback, stag beetle, wisps, pixies, ember foxes, grumpy oak, spiderlings, talking trees
canon: soft
text: The Whisperwood's lesser folk: bristly bramble ferrets that rob foragers' bags, bristlebacks (quilled bears, slow and easily offended), stag beetles the size of ponies, pixies who keep a toll stone, riddling wisps and hollow oaks, oaks that take a stubbed toe personally, coin-sized spiderlings by the hundred in the deep wood, and foxes with ember tails who will walk a polite student through. The old trees gossip about the students who walk under them.

@lore elder_oak
keys: elder oak, pale ring, the cold spring
canon: soft
text: The oldest tree in the Whisperwood, in the heartwood, with the other trees grown round it like courtiers and a face in its bark with its eyes shut. Asked politely, it shows its rings: sixty in from the bark, one is pale as frost, for "the cold spring". It gives a splinter of heartwood to someone it judges worthy.

@lore masked_circle
keys: masked circle, white masks, chalk circle, Compel
canon: soft
text: Three hedge-dabblers in plain white masks who practise Compel in a chalk circle in the heartwood. Their chalk marks are copied, badly, from somewhere; they don't know what the marks are for. Absalom Vrell taught them, after a fashion.

@lore wood_witch
keys: wood's witch, ghost on the log, last witch
canon: soft
text: A ghost who knits nothing on a fallen log in the Hush. She was the wood's witch before the castle, before the lake was a lake, and left when the Spinner came; she still can't decide if that was cowardice or manners. She knows the Spinner's weakness.

@lore village_tailor
keys: village tailor, ward thread, ward-stitched cloak
canon: soft
text: The tailor on the village square, who exchanges students' ruined robes at half price and, it's said, feels it whenever a sleeve is singed. She sews Sister Amaranth's ward thread into cloaks, and will make a robe of Spinner silk, at a price "for my nerves".

@lore pawnbroker
keys: pawnbroker, stuffed owl, loupe
canon: soft
text: The village pawnbroker, under a stuffed owl, turns each thing twice through his loupe as if it hid a second, cheaper thing underneath. He buys back what students are tired of.
